## Optimizing for Memory vs. Space

Below is a video with a very comprehensive showcase of all three approaces:
[Trial Division](#1-trial-division),
[Sieve of Eratosthenes](#2-sieve-of-eratosthenes), and
[Dijkstra's Approach](#3-dijkstras-approach).

<YouTube id="fwxjMKBMR7s" title="Finding Primes - b001" />

### 1. Trial Division

The most memory-efficient but slowest method. It relies on the fact
that divisors are mirrored around the square root of <Math math={n} />.

* **Time**: <Math math={sqrtN} /> \<\<= on theory, good, in reality the slowness is
  due to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Integer_factorization">Integer
  Factorization</a>.
* **Space**: <Math math={o1} />

### 2. Sieve of Eratosthenes

The quickest for bulk evaluation.

* **Time**: <Math math={sieveTime} />
* **Space**: <Math math={oN} /> (requires a boolean array up to <Math math={n} />)

### 3. Dijkstra's Approach

A balanced approach using a "pool" of tuples to track multiples, avoiding the
massive memory hit of the Sieve while staying faster than Trial Division.

* **Time**: <Math math={sieveTime} /> \<\<= whilst sharing the same time
  complexity as [Sieve of Eratosthenes](#2-sieve-of-eratosthenes), it usually
  performs slower due to
  <a href="https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/computer-organization-architecture/locality-of-reference-and-cache-operation-in-cache-memory/">
  Cache Locality</a>.
* **Space**: <Math math={oN} /> (requires a boolean array up to <Math math={n} />)

---

## Not only Big O

Big O describes the **worst-case scenario**. Mathematically:

<img src={bigOExample} alt="Big O Chart" />

<p class="text-center">
  <Math math={bigODef} />
</p>

### Big Omega (<Math math={omega} />)

Describes the **best-case scenario** (lower bound):

<img src={bigOmegaExample} alt="Big Omega Chart" />

<p class="text-center">
  <Math math={bigOmegaDef} />
</p>

### Big Theta (<Math math={theta} />)

Describes the **tightest bound** (average case):

<img src={bigThetaExample} alt="Big Theta Chart" />

<p class="text-center">
  <Math math={bigThetaDef} />
</p>

---

## Summary

* **Big O** is like <Math math={leq} /> (Worst case / Upper bound)
* **Big <Math math={omega} />** is like <Math math={geq} /> (Best case / Lower bound)
* **Big <Math math={theta} />** is like `==` (Tightly bounded / Average case)

Determining what to optimize depends on your specific use case. A big factor to
these algorithms is also input size, sometimes algorithms like bubble sort
outperform ones like quicksort due to better cache locality (and aggressive
compiler optimization because it's a more predictable pattern) on small input
sizes.
